[38;5;231m⏺[39m Tests it is — [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, no linter.

  That choice raises one loose end I don't want to resolve behind your back. You chose a [1mbrowser global[0m module shape,
  but Node can't [38;5;153mrequire[39m a browser-global IIFE, so testing [38;5;153mdetectEnvironment[39m needs a seam. Two ways, and they differ in
  whether production code changes:

  - [1mCommonJS export guard[0m — 3 test-only lines at the bottom of [38;5;153msettings.js[39m ([38;5;153mif (typeof module !== "undefined") [39m
    [38;5;153mmodule.exports = { detectEnvironment, ENVIRONMENTS }[39m), ignored by browsers. Buys: dead simple, conventional, tests
    import the module normally. Costs: reintroduces a sliver of the dual-format shape you just declined, and the tested
    surface is the export, not quite what the browser loads.
  - [1mLoad the file in [38;5;153mnode:vm[0m — the test reads [38;5;153msettings.js[39m as text and runs it against a fake [38;5;153mwindow[39m/[38;5;153mlocation[39m. Buys:
    production file stays purely browser-shaped, and tests exercise byte-for-byte what ships, including the IIFE. Costs:
    ~10 lines of stdlib test scaffolding, slightly indirect to read.

  I lean toward the [1mexport guard[0m — it's the plainer of the two, and your instructions favor clear over clever. But the
  [38;5;153mvm[39m option is the one that actually honors "browser global, nothing else," so it's a fair call either way.

